>
  <header class="doc-head">
    <h1>Execution model: three tiers, zero forks</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      POSIX <code>fork()</code> has no equivalent on Win32. MSYS2/Cygwin's answer is to
      <strong>fabricate</strong> it at the syscall layer — expensive, fragile, and famous for its
      quirks. niubash's engine (rubash) takes a different road: <strong>replicate fork's semantics,
      not its mechanism</strong>. The result: subshells and command substitution at zero
      process-creation cost, while everything a script can observe — exit codes, fd inheritance,
      shared offsets, signal disposition — matches GNU Bash.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2>The three-tier model</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Which face of fork it covers</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>1. In-process subshells</strong></td>
          <td><code>( list )</code> and <code>$( )</code> never create a process: <code>ShellState::clone</code> copies variables, aliases, functions, traps and history; the subshell ends and the copy is discarded.</td>
          <td>fork's copied "memory face".</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>2. A real-handle fd table</strong></td>
          <td>Slots hold native Windows <code>HANDLE</code>s; handles duplicated via <code>DuplicateHandle</code> share the same kernel file object — hence shared file offsets, exactly POSIX "dup shares the open file description" (proven with a POC before landing). <code>fork_table()</code> copies the whole table handle by handle.</td>
          <td>fork copies the fd table but not the file object.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>3. Real processes only at boundaries</strong></td>
          <td>External commands and pipeline members start via <code>CreateProcess</code> + <code>os_pipe</code>; background jobs and coprocs each hold a real process, job control builds on Job Objects, SIGCONT goes through <code>ResumeThread</code>.</td>
          <td>Where fork is genuinely unavoidable: the process boundary.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      In other words: what can avoid creating a process (subshells, command substitution) never
      creates one; what must create a process (external commands, pipelines, background jobs) takes
      Win32's native path directly. No "simulated POSIX" layer sits in between.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>62 real builtins + 12 inlined coreutils + pipeline-inline arms</h2>
    <p>
      Command words dispatch in three tiers. <strong>62 real builtins</strong> execute in-process
      (all <strong>61 of GNU Bash 5.3's builtins covered, none missing</strong>, plus an in-process
      <code>env</code> — coreutils semantics, sorted output, <code>-0</code> support). There are also
      hidden fast-paths (<code>sleep</code>/<code>dirname</code>/<code>basename</code>/<code>uname</code>/<code>arch</code>):
      simple argument forms run in-process, complex forms fall back to the external command, and
      introspection (<code>type</code>/<code>command -v</code>/<code>enable</code>) reports them as
      <strong>external commands — same as GNU Bash reports</strong>.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>In-process tier</th><th>Roster</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Inlined file commands (12)</td>
          <td><code>pwd</code> <code>printf</code> <code>mkdir</code> <code>touch</code> <code>chmod</code> <code>cp</code> <code>rm</code> <code>rmdir</code> <code>cat</code> <code>sed</code> <code>mkfifo</code> <code>tty</code></td>
          <td>High-frequency file commands execute in-process inside the engine (aligned to GNU coreutils stdout/rc; complex forms with flags fall back to the external command).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Pipeline-inline arms</td>
          <td><code>true</code> <code>:</code> <code>false</code> <code>echo</code> <code>printf</code> <code>trap</code> <code>head</code> <code>cat</code> <code>sed</code> <code>grep</code> <code>wc</code> <code>tr</code></td>
          <td>Matched by the command's <strong>position in the pipeline</strong>: a hit runs in-process; only refused forms (e.g. grep regex metacharacters) fall back to the external binary.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Combined, a pipeline like <code>echo | cat</code> is <strong>entirely zero-process</strong>.
      The roster is not a documentation slogan: the builtin whitelist and the fast-path list are
      guarded by cargo-test documentation-sync tests — change either side of code or docs without
      the other and the build fails.
    </p>
    <details class="zh-fold">
      <summary>中文原文 · Chinese original</summary>
      <div lang="zh-CN">
        <p>
          命令词按三层分发。<strong>62 个真内建</strong>进程内执行（GNU Bash 5.3 的
          61 个内建<strong>全覆盖、无缺失</strong>，外加进程内 <code>env</code>——
          coreutils 语义、排序输出、支持 <code>-0</code>）。另有隐藏 fast-path
          （<code>sleep</code>/<code>dirname</code>/<code>basename</code>/<code>uname</code>/<code>arch</code>）：
          简单参数形态进程内执行，复杂形态回退外部命令，而 introspection
          （<code>type</code>/<code>command -v</code>/<code>enable</code>）报告为
          <strong>外部命令——与 GNU Bash 的报告一致</strong>。
        </p>
        <p>
          组合起来，<code>echo | cat</code> 这样的管道<strong>一整个零进程</strong>。
          名单不是文档口号：内建白名单与 fast-path 清单由 <code>cargo test</code> 的
          文档同步测试守护——代码与文档任何一侧变动，另一侧不改则构建失败。
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Observable behavior: zero difference, proven</h2>
    <p>
      "Implemented differently" is not a euphemism for "behaves differently". The observable surface
      of this model — exit code propagation, fd inheritance and shared offsets, signal disposition —
      is exactly what GNU's 83 upstream test corpora cover: 83/83 passing, 82 suites byte-identical
      (see the <a href="/en/docs/compatibility/">compatibility overview</a>). As corroborating
      evidence, the engine also runs the core loop of the bashdb debugger end-to-end
      (list/step/next/where/continue/quit) and git's own test framework <code>t/test-lib.sh</code> —
      both extremely sensitive to execution-model details.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the performance payoff of this design (subshells, command substitution, background jobs)
      and the honest parity floor of external processes, see
      <a href="/en/docs/performance-model/">the performance profile</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

